            "Ghana's maternal mortality — around 260 to 310 per 100,000 live births — is made of haemorrhage, hypertensive disease, sepsis, unsafe abortion and the indirect deaths of anaemia, sickle cell disease, cardiac disease and malaria, and the women who die in intensive care usually arrive late from a district facility, already in multi-organ failure. Pregnancy rewrites the ICU's numbers. <strong>Oxygen reserve</strong> is small: the functional residual capacity falls 20 per cent and oxygen consumption rises, so the pregnant woman desaturates within a minute of apnoea and pre-oxygenation before intubation is meticulous. The <strong>airway</strong> is harder: mucosal oedema, breast enlargement, a full stomach from the first trimester onward and aspiration risk mean a smaller tube (6.0–7.0 mm), the most experienced intubator, a rapid sequence induction and a surgical airway plan. The <strong>circulation</strong> is bigger: blood volume up 40–50 per cent, cardiac output up 40 per cent, resting heart rate up 10–15, blood pressure lower in the second trimester — so she loses 1,200 to 1,500 mL before her pressure falls, and the fetus is starved of perfusion long before the mother looks shocked. From 20 weeks the gravid uterus compresses the vena cava and aorta when she lies flat: <strong>every supine pregnant woman is tilted 15–30 degrees to the left or the uterus is displaced manually</strong>, in the bed, on the trolley and during CPR. The normal <strong>blood gas</strong> is a compensated respiratory alkalosis — PaCO₂ 3.7–4.3 kPa (28–32 mmHg), bicarbonate 18–21 — so a 'normal' PaCO₂ of 5.3 kPa in a pregnant asthmatic is respiratory failure, and ventilation targets a PaCO₂ of about 4–4.5 kPa to maintain the gradient that clears fetal CO₂. The <strong>kidneys</strong> clear more, so a creatinine of 90 micromol/L is abnormal; the <strong>clotting</strong> is hypercoagulable, so low-molecular-weight heparin prophylaxis is standard for every critically ill pregnant or postpartum woman. Drugs cross the placenta and enter milk: most ICU drugs are used when the mother needs them — the fetus does best when the mother is well — with ACE inhibitors, warfarin in early pregnancy, tetracyclines, and NSAIDs after 30 weeks avoided, and the obstetrician, neonatologist and pharmacist consulted. The fetus is monitored (a daily or continuous CTG from 26–28 weeks when the plan would change on the findings; the fetal heart as the earliest monitor of maternal perfusion), and the question 'does delivery help the mother?' is asked daily — in pre-eclampsia, HELLP, fatty liver and often in ARDS, it does.",
