        "Fluids: 10 mL/kg boluses reassessed each time, up to 40–60 mL/kg in septic shock with early adrenaline or noradrenaline, but no routine boluses in the febrile African child without hypotension (FEAST) — blood for severe anaemia and maintenance for severe malaria instead; Plan C 100 mL/kg Ringer's as 30 + 70 by age; maintenance 4-2-1 with isotonic dextrose-containing fluid (restricted in meningitis and pneumonia); ReSoMal, F-75 and small watched volumes in severe malnutrition; packed cells 10–15 mL/kg, urgently in severe malarial anaemia.",
        "By problem: bubble CPAP and ampicillin–gentamicin for severe pneumonia; dexamethasone and nebulised adrenaline for croup with no throat examination in epiglottitis; supportive care only for bronchiolitis; access within 5 minutes (intraosseous after two failures), antibiotics within the hour and early vasoactive infusions for septic shock; the status epilepticus clock with glucose, benzodiazepine twice, phenytoin or levetiracetam, then intubation; DKA fluids over 48 hours with hourly neurological observations; and ventilation at 6–8 mL/kg with pressure-controlled modes, small circuits and DOPES, with the parent at the bedside.",
        "Pregnancy changes the numbers: desaturation within a minute of apnoea, a difficult airway needing a smaller tube and rapid sequence induction, 1,200–1,500 mL lost before the pressure falls, left uterine displacement in every supine woman from 20 weeks, a normal PaCO₂ of 28–32 mmHg and bicarbonate 18–21, a creatinine of 90 abnormal, hypercoagulability needing heparin prophylaxis, drugs used when the mother needs them with the obstetrician and pharmacist consulted, the fetal heart as the earliest perfusion monitor, and the daily question of whether delivery helps.",
        "Severe pre-eclampsia: delivery is the cure; magnesium sulphate 4 g IV then 1 g/hour (or Pritchard 10 g IM then 5 g four-hourly) for 24 hours after delivery or the last fit, with reflexes, respiratory rate and four-hourly urine output checked before each dose and calcium gluconate at the bedside; pressure lowered to 140–150/90–100 with labetalol, hydralazine (with preload) or nifedipine; fluids restricted to 80 mL/hour because pulmonary oedema kills; HELLP needs platelets above 50 for delivery and vigilance for liver haematoma; eclampsia is managed by protection, side-lying, magnesium and delivery after stabilisation, with 44 per cent of fits after delivery.",
        "PPH is the 4 Ts treated by massage, oxytocin, ergometrine (not in hypertension), misoprostol, carboprost (not in asthma), tranexamic acid within 3 hours, bladder emptying, the massive transfusion protocol with early fibrinogen, balloon tamponade, compression and surgery; puerperal sepsis and septic abortion get the sepsis bundle within the hour and source control within hours, without judgement; amniotic fluid embolism is sudden collapse then DIC managed with CPR, oxygen, vasopressors, massive transfusion and hysterectomy; peripartum cardiomyopathy is heart failure care with anticoagulation and contraception counselling.",
        "Maternal arrest: ALS with manual left uterine displacement, hands slightly higher, early expert intubation, access above the diaphragm, calcium for magnesium toxicity and a perimortem caesarean started by 4 minutes and completed by 5 minutes where she arrested from 20 weeks; afterwards the baby is brought to the mother, lactation is maintained by three-hourly expressing with drug compatibility checked rather than assumed, heparin prophylaxis and anti-D are given, contraception and pre-pregnancy counselling planned, the family's decision-makers identified and documented, and every death and near-miss reviewed through Maternal Death Surveillance and Response for the three delays.",
