          body: "RSI: checklist read aloud, two suctions, a bougie, capnography confirmation (the only reliable test), pre-oxygenation 3–5 minutes with nasal cannulae left on, a pressor drawn up, ketamine in shock, suxamethonium 1.5 mg/kg (not in hyperkalaemia, burns after 48 hours, neuromuscular disease) or rocuronium 1.2 mg/kg, no more than three attempts, then supraglottic then front of neck. Central line bundle: hand hygiene, full barrier, 2 per cent chlorhexidine–alcohol, subclavian or jugular over femoral, daily review, removal when not needed; scrub the hub; dressing every 7 days. Catheter: closed system, bag below the bladder, daily review, no asymptomatic bacteriuria treatment. Intraosseous after two failed attempts, same doses, under pressure, out within 24 hours. LP: L3/4, contraindications before, tubes labelled in order, antibiotics never delayed. Tracheostomy: inner cannula 4–8-hourly, humidification always, cuff 20–30 cmH₂O, two people for ties and turns, first change not before day 4–7; emergency — oxygen to face and stoma, remove the valve and inner cannula, pass a catheter, deflate the cuff, remove the tube, oxygenate by face or stoma, reintubate; laryngectomy breathes only through the stoma. NG tube: pH 5.5 or below and X-ray, never the whoosh test. High-alert drugs: independent double check, labelled syringes and lines, a dedicated vasopressor lumen, concentrated potassium locked away, controlled drug discrepancies reported in writing immediately. Pressure: two-hourly turns, heels floated, devices moved daily, dark skin assessed by touch and pain. Eyes: lubricant and a moisture chamber. VTE: LMWH or mechanical, daily. Mobilise from day one. Transport: oxygen for the journey plus 30 minutes, cylinder minutes = litres ÷ flow, ventilator trialled before leaving, ISBAR on arrival.",
