        "Rapid sequence induction in the ICU is prepared by a checklist read aloud — two suctions, bag-valve-mask with PEEP, tubes and a smaller one, bougie, laryngoscopes, supraglottic airway, surgical airway kit, tested capnography, assigned roles — with 3–5 minutes of pre-oxygenation head-up and apnoeic nasal oxygen, fluid and a push-dose pressor ready, ketamine (shock, asthma), propofol or etomidate, then suxamethonium 1.5 mg/kg (avoided in hyperkalaemia, burns and crush after 48 hours, neuromuscular disease, organophosphates) or rocuronium 1.2 mg/kg, confirmation by waveform capnography, immediate sedation, and a failed-airway plan of three attempts, supraglottic airway, then front-of-neck access.",
        "Central lines: hand hygiene, maximal barrier precautions, 2 per cent chlorhexidine in alcohol, ultrasound, subclavian or jugular over femoral, a checklist the nurse can stop the procedure for, X-ray before use, hub scrubbed for 15 seconds, dressing changed every 7 days, sets every 4–7 days (24 hours for lipid and propofol), a daily 'still needed?' review, and removal supine with firm pressure and an occlusive dressing; urinary catheters only for true indications, closed system, bag below the bladder, no routine changes or treatment of asymptomatic bacteriuria, and daily review; intraosseous access after two failed attempts with all drugs at IV doses under pressure, lidocaine for pain, removed within 24 hours.",
