          alt: "Breaking bad news, and the family who ask you not to. A structure that works: find a private place and sit down; ask what she already understands; ask how much she wants to know; give a warning, then the news, in plain words; stop talking — let the silence do its work; respond to the feeling before the facts; agree what happens next, and say you will return. Most of it is asking and waiting, not telling. 'Please do not tell her': do not agree and do not overrule them; ask what they are afraid will happen; explain that you will not lie to her and will not force information on her — you will follow her own questions; most families accept this. Care that continues when treatment stops: a decision not to attempt resuscitation is a decision about one intervention only; pain relief, mouth care, positioning, company and honest answers all continue and usually intensify; never write or say that there is nothing more to be done. After the death, in a Ghanaian setting: loud, public, communal grieving is normal and healthy, not a breakdown to be sedated; give the family a private space and time with the body; ask about washing and religious rites before you proceed; explain the mortuary and documentation steps once, slowly, then write them down.",
          caption: "The ICU family meeting is this structure with a team behind it: agree the message first, ask before telling, warn, say it plainly, be silent, answer the feeling, agree a plan and document it. A DNACPR decision is about one intervention; everything else — and especially the nursing — continues.",
